---INSTRUCTION---
Write a function `cells_in_range/1` that takes a string in the format "A1:B2" representing a rectangular range of cells on an Excel sheet. The function must return a list of all cell names within that range, inclusive of the start and end cells.

The input string will always have a single uppercase letter for the column and a single digit for the row in each cell name (e.g., "A1", "B2"). The range is defined by the two cell names separated by a colon.

The returned list must be in column-major order: for each column in the range from the start column to the end column, include all rows from the start row to the end row for that column, then proceed to the next column. For example, given "A1:B2", the output should be ["A1", "A2", "B1", "B2"].

The function must handle the case where the start and end cells are identical, returning a single-element list.

---TEST---
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "basic range A1:B2" do
    assert Solution.cells_in_range("A1:B2") == ["A1", "A2", "B1", "B2"]
  end

  test "another range E5:F6" do
    assert Solution.cells_in_range("E5:F6") == ["E5", "E6", "F5", "F6"]
  end

  test "range C3:D4" do
    assert Solution.cells_in_range("C3:D4") == ["C3", "C4", "D3", "D4"]
  end

  test "single cell range" do
    assert Solution.cells_in_range("A1:A1") == ["A1"]
  end
end

---REFERENCE---
defmodule Solution do
  def cells_in_range(s) do
    [start_cell, end_cell] = String.split(s, ":")
    <<start_col::utf8, start_row_str::binary>> = start_cell
    <<end_col::utf8, end_row_str::binary>> = end_cell
    start_row = String.to_integer(start_row_str)
    end_row = String.to_integer(end_row_str)

    for col <- start_col..end_col, row <- start_row..end_row do
      <<col::utf8>> <> Integer.to_string(row)
    end
  end
end
